<Project>

  <!--
    Self-heal a root-owned NuGet home before restore reads user settings.

    Some verify / CI hosts provision "$HOME/.nuget" (or its "NuGet" settings
    subdirectory) owned by root before the unprivileged build user runs. NuGet
    loads the user-global settings from "$HOME/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config" during
    restore — ahead of any repository nuget.config, MSBuild RestoreConfigFile, or
    environment override — so an unreadable one aborts every `dotnet restore` /
    `build` / `test` before a single source file compiles:

      error : Failed to read NuGet.Config due to unauthorized access.
              Path: '<home>/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config'.
              Access to the path '<home>/.nuget/NuGet' is denied. Permission denied

    No committed build *input* can redirect that read, but a target that runs
    *before* the read can repair the home. This target invokes the vetted,
    idempotent scripts/prepare-nuget-home.sh to relocate an unusable home so the
    build user gets a writable one. It is a fast no-op when the home is already
    usable (the normal developer / CI case). The invocation is best-effort
    (`|| true`): a genuinely unremediable host (e.g. "$HOME" itself not writable,
    where the script exits non-zero for its operator callers) then falls through
    to NuGet's own clear "unauthorized access" error instead of failing the build
    on the helper. `|| true` — rather than MSBuild ContinueOnError — is used so
    the step never emits an MSBuild warning that `/warnaserror` would promote to
    an error. See docs/build-environment.md §1.

    Scoped to non-Windows because the script and the "$HOME/.nuget" layout it
    repairs are POSIX-specific; Windows uses %APPDATA%\NuGet.
  -->
  <Target Name="PrepareNuGetHome"
          Condition="'$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT'"
          BeforeTargets="Restore;_GenerateRestoreGraph;_GenerateRestoreGraphProjectEntry;_GetRestoreSettings;CollectPackageReferences">
    <Exec Command="bash &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)scripts/prepare-nuget-home.sh&quot; || true"
          StandardOutputImportance="Low"
          StandardErrorImportance="Low" />
  </Target>

</Project>
